{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And they plotted against him to kill him.</b> The term נכל (plot) denotes devising evil, as in אֲשֶׁר נִכְּלוּ לָכֶם (Numbers 25:18). He says that in their hearts they imagined Joseph to be plotting to kill them, and that he came to them not to inquire after their welfare but to find a pretext against them or to cause them to sin, so that their father would curse them or the blessed El would punish them, and he alone would remain blessed among the sons. The hitpa'el form denotes forming a conception of the thing in the soul, as in אַתָּה מִתְנַקֵשׁ בְּנַפְשִׁי (1 Samuel 28:9) — you form in your heart a snare against my soul. And the infinitive לַהֲמִיתוֹ — that he would put his brothers to death, as in לַעֲשׂוֹתְכֶם אוֹתָם (Deuteronomy 4:14), לְעָבְרְךָ בִּבְרִית (Deuteronomy 29:11). By this he indicates what overcame them despite all of them being fully righteous, so that their names were before YHWH for a remembrance — how they joined heart together to kill their brother or to sell him, and did not relent from the evil. For even when they said אֲבָל אֲשֵׁמִים אֲנַחְנוּ עַל אָחִינוּ (Genesis 42:21), they did not say that their guilt lay in selling him or putting him to death, but in their cruelty בְּהִתְחַנְּנוֹ. Thus Scripture informs us that they formed in their hearts and reckoned Joseph to be plotting and ensnaring their lives, to kill them — in this world or in the world to come or in both — and the Torah states: הַבָּא לְהָרְגְךָ etc."], :book :genesis, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:33:43.946312500Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}